.TH "APKBUILD" "5" "2026-08-02"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
\fBAPKBUILD\fR - metadata and instructions to build packages
.PP
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
An \fBAPKBUILD\fR file is used by tools such as abuild(1) to build packages for
eventual installation by the apk(8) package manager.\& It defines metadata such
as the name of packages, the version information, the source license,
and contact information for the developer.\& It additionally contains the
commands needed to build, test, and install files.\&
.PP
.SS Syntax and Environment
.PP
The \fBAPKBUILD\fR format is based on the shell command language as specified
in IEEE Std 1003.\&1-2017 ("POSIX.\&1-2017").\& The \fBAPKBUILD\fR is sourced into
a prepared shell environment (or evaluated in a way that is equivalent).\&
The resulting shell environment is referred to as "global environment".\&
The variables and functions set in the global environment are used by for
example abuild(1) to create packages.\&
.PP
Depending on the implementation different shell extensions might be available
or allowed.\& The abuild(1) utility as distributed by Alpine Linux uses the
BusyBox Almquist shell, a part of busybox(1) that is currently undocumented.\&
It is mostly POSIX.\&1-2017 compliant, with some bash-like extensions.\& The
abuild(1) utility as distributed by Adélie uses the user'\&s preferred /bin/sh,
which may be bash(1) or dash(1).\&
.PP
After an \fBAPKBUILD\fR was sourced or an implemented function was run the
shell environment should not be changed in ways not explicitly allowed
here.\& For example it should not return with a changed \fBumask\fR, \fBset -f\fR
or \fBIFS\fR.\& When an \fBAPKBUILD\fR is sourced it should not use external
commands, predefined functions or source additional files.\&
.PP
When an \fBAPKBUILD\fR is sourced or an implemented function is run the
shell environment may be assumed to use \fBset -e\fR and \fBumask 0022\fR.\&
.PP
.SS Variables
.PP
The following variables may be set in the global environment.\& Unless
otherwise specified these variables must not be changed after the
\fBAPKBUILD\fR was initially sourced.\& An \fBAPKBUILD\fR may assume these variables
to be unset/empty when the \fBAPKBUILD\fR is sourced.\& Unless otherwise specified
a "list" variable has it'\&s elements seperated by space, tab and/or newline characters
(same as the default \fBIFS\fR value).\& Unless otherwise specified the values
of variables may vary depending on the value of predefined variables.\&
.PP
An \fBAPKBUILD\fR may set other variables but their name must either start
with an underscore, start with an uppercase character and be exported
or be set with the \fBlocal\fR shell extension.\&
.PP
\fBmaintainer\fR
.RS 4
The name and email of the \fBAPKBUILD\fR maintainer in RFC 2822 format.\&
This may be set through a maintainer comment instead:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
# Maintainer: Joe Q\&. Public <john\&.q\&.public@example\&.com>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RE
\fBpkgname\fR
.RS 4
Specifies the name of the main package.\& Must be set to a valid package
name according to apk-package(5).\& This is typically the name of the
package upstream; however, note that all letters should be lowercased.\&
.PP
Libraries for scripting languages should have a prefix before the
library name describing the language.\& Such prefixes include \fIlua-\fR,
\fIperl-\fR, \fIpy3-\fR, and \fIrb-\fR.\& Not all languages use prefixes.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBpkgver\fR
.RS 4
Must be set to a non-empty value that specifies the version of the
software being packaged.\& The version of a package must consist of one
or more numbers separated by the radix (decimal point).\& The final
number may have a single letter following it, for upstreams that use
such a versioning scheme (such as 1.\&5a, 1.\&5b, 1.\&5c).\&
.PP
After the final number (and optional single letter), one or more
suffixes may be appended, which must be an underscore (_) followed
by one of \fIalpha\fR, \fIbeta\fR, \fIpre\fR, \fIrc\fR, \fIcvs\fR, \fIsvn\fR, \fIgit\fR, \fIhg\fR, or
\fIp\fR, optionally followed by another number.\& If a suffix is \fIalpha\fR,
\fIbeta\fR, \fIpre\fR, or \fIrc\fR, it is considered to be earlier than the
version without this suffix; if a suffix is \fIcvs\fR, \fIsvn, \fRgit_, \fIhg\fR,
or \fIp\fR, it is considered to be later than the version without this
suffix.\& All of the following examples are valid versions, in order
from lowest to highest:
.PP
1.\&0, 1.\&1_alpha2, 1.\&1.\&3_pre, 1.\&1.\&3_pre_p2, 1.\&1.\&3, 1.\&1.\&3_hg, 1.\&2, 1.\&2a, 1.\&2b
.PP
.RE
\fBpkgrel\fR
.RS 4
Must be set to an unsigned integer that specifies the \fBAPKBUILD\fR release
number of this particular \fBAPKBUILD\fR version.\& This indicates when a
\fBAPKBUILD\fR has changed without a corresponding change in version.\& Always
increment \fBpkgrel\fR when the resulting packages change.\& The first
release of a \fBAPKBUILD\fR is always 0.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBpkgdesc\fR
.RS 4
Must be set to a non-empty value the specifies what the package
contains.\& \fBpkgdesc\fR must be 128 characters or less, and should
concisely describe what actions the software or items being package
will allow the user to perform.\& For example,
"Fully-featured word processor with spell check and plugins"
would be a sufficient \fBpkgdesc\fR for AbiWord.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBurl\fR
.RS 4
Must be set to a non-empty value that specifies the Web address of
the package'\&s upstream.\& This should allow users and future maintainers
to find documentation, release information, and contact information
for the package.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBarch\fR
.RS 4
Specifies on which architectures the \fBAPKBUILD\fR may be built.\& It
is highly recommended that you set this variable to "\fIall\fR" if the
software is portable.\&
.PP
\fBarch\fR may be either a disallow list that includes one of \fIall\fR or
\fInoarch\fR and the rest being architecture names prefixed with \fI!\&\fR.\&
E.\&g.\& \fIarch="all !\&ppc64le"\fR means that the \fBAPKBUILD\fR is allowed to be
built on all architectures except for ppc64le.\&
.PP
When \fInoarch\fR is used instead of \fIall\fR it indicates that a package does
not contain any architecture specific files.\& Such packages may include
pure Python packages, shell script packages, and JARs.\& If you are not
sure what this means, using \fIall\fR is safe.\&
.PP
Alternatively \fBarch\fR may be an allow list that includes a list of
architecture names.\& Consequently when \fBarch\fR is empty the \fBAPKBUILD\fR
is not enabled on any arch.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBlicense\fR
.RS 4
Specifies the license under which the package is distributed.\& It
must be set to a SPDX license expression.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBdepends\fR
.RS 4
List of run-time dependencies of the package.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBdepends_lang\fR, \fBdepends_doc\fR, \fBdepends_dev\fR, \fBdepends_static\fR,
\fBdepends_libs\fR, \fBdepends_openrc\fR, \fBdepends_systemd\fR, \fBdepends_udev\fR
.RS 4
Default \fBdepends\fR value used by the predefined functions named
\fBdefault_${name#depends_}\fR (eg.\& \fBdefault_lang\fR uses \fBdepends="$depends_lang"\fR).\&
These variables should not be used when the respective function is not
(directly or indirectly) used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBmakedepends\fR
.RS 4
List of build dependencies for the package.\& Defaults to
\fB$makedepends_build $makedepends_host\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBmakedepends_build\fR
.RS 4
List of build dependencies for the \fBCBUILD\fR machine, installed
without \fB--root\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBmakedepends_host\fR
.RS 4
List of build dependencies for the \fBCHOST\fR machine, installed into
\fBCBUILDROOT\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBcheckdepends\fR
.RS 4
List of test-time dependencies of the package.\& Common packages that
are used for testing include check, dejagnu, and perl-test-command.\&
Installed if \fBcheck\fR will be run (see \fBwant_check\fR).\&
.PP
.RE
\fBpkggroups\fR
.RS 4
List of login groups to create during build-time.\& Note that you will
need to create the login groups in a pre-install script as well; see
\fIInstall Scripts\fR for more information about install scripts.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBpkgusers\fR
.RS 4
List of user logins to create during build-time.\& Note that you will
need to create the user logins in a pre-install install script as well;
see \fIInstall Scripts\fR for more information about install scripts.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBprovides\fR
.RS 4
List of providers that the package may be referred to in addition
to \fB$pkgname=$pkgver-r$pkgrel\fR.\&
.PP
Specifying a provider with version such as \fIfoobar=1.\&2\fR will
cause the package to be an "alias" of \fIfoobar\fR version \fI1.\&2\fR.\& It
will be automatically installed if a user then runs `apk add foobar`
or similar, and it will conflict with a package named \fIfoobar\fR.\&
.PP
Specifying a provider without a version such as \fIbaz\fR will
cause the package to provide a "virtual" called \fIbaz\fR.\& Multiple
packages with the same virtual provider can be installed on a system;
however, if a user runs `apk add baz` they will be provided a list
of packages that provide \fIbaz\fR and must select one and install it.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBprovider_priority\fR
.RS 4
An unsigned integer for apk(8) to use when considering which provider
should be installed for the same \fBprovides\fR virtual provider.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBreplaces\fR
.RS 4
List of packages that the package should replace files from when
both are installed.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBreplaces_priority\fR
.RS 4
Specifies an unsigned integer that is used by apk(8) when multiple
packages with \fBreplaces\fR include the same file.\& It is also used
to decide which package should define the permissions of a directory
even without \fBreplaces\fR set.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBinstall_if\fR
.RS 4
List of conditions when apk(8) should automatically install the
(sub)package.\& For instance, the \fBdefault_openrc\fR split function set
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
install_if="openrc ${subpkgname%-openrc}=$pkgver-r$pkgrel"
.fi
.RE
.PP
which means that the subpackage will be automatically installed if
both \fBopenrc\fR and the \fB${subpkgname%-openrc}\fR package are installed.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBinstall\fR
.RS 4
List of install scripts for the package, if any.\& See \fIInstall
Scripts\fR for more information about install scripts.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBtriggers\fR
.RS 4
List of trigger scripts used by the specified (sub)packages.\& A
trigger script is a shell script that is called whenever monitored
files or directories are modified.\& You may specify the paths to
monitor using the triggers variable as follows:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
$pkgname\&.trigger=/usr/share/man:/usr/local/share/man
.fi
.RE
.PP
This will run the trigger script for \fI$pkgname\fR whenever files in
\fI/usr/share/man\fR or \fI/usr/local/share/man\fR are created, modified,
or removed.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsubpackages\fR
.RS 4
List of subpackage descriptions with the following syntax:
\fBsubpkgname\fR[:[\fBsplitfunc\fR][:[all|noarch|\fBtriplet\fR]]] 
.br
Typically, this will include \fI$pkgname-dev\fR for development
files (such as \fI/usr/include\fR and pc(5) files) and
\fI$pkgname-doc\fR for documentation (such as \fI/usr/share/doc\fR and
\fI/usr/share/man\fR).\&
.PP
By default the split function name is the end of the package name after
the last hyphen.\& Eg.\& for \fI$pkgname-foo\fR and \fIlibfoo\fR the split functions
are \fIfoo\fR and \fIlibfoo\fR respectively.\& When the package name ends with
\fB-bash-completion\fR, \fB-zsh-completion\fR or \fB-fish-completion\fR the default
split function name is \fBbashcomp\fR, \fBzshcomp\fR, \fBfishcomp\fR respectively
instead.\& A custom split function name may be set by using \fIfoo:funcname\fR.\&
.PP
By default \fBarch\fR determines whether or not a subpackage is considered
arch independant or not.\& Subpackages ending in \fB-doc\fR, \fB-openrc\fR,
\fB-lang\fR, \fBsh-completion\fR, \fB-pyc\fR, \fB-nftrules\fR, \fB-systemd\fR or \fB-udev\fR
default to being arch independant regardless of \fBarch\fR.\& This may be
changed by using \fIfoo::noarch\fR or \fIfoo:funcname:all\fR.\&
.PP
If mandatory a subpackage may be specified with \fIfoo::$CBUILD\fR or
\fIfoo::$CTARGET\fR to specify that the subpackage is architecture
dependant and not for \fBCHOST\fR.\& This is should normally not be used.\&
.PP
When multiple subpackages are specified their split functions are
executed in the order in which they occur in \fBsubpackages\fR.\&
.PP
When used the \fI$pkgname-dbg\fR subpackage should be the first one
and \fI$pkgname-static\fR should be placed before \fI$pkgname-dev\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsource\fR
.RS 4
List of local and remote files.\& Local files are specified through
the file name and should be present in the same directory as the
\fBAPKBUILD\fR.\& Remote files are specified by an URL using the http, https
or ftp scheme which is optionally prefixed with \fBfilename::\fR.\& The
default file name for remote files is the text after the last slash
in the URL.\& The resulting value must not vary depending on the
environment (eg.\& \fBCHOST\fR) that the \fBAPKBUILD\fR was sourced with.\&
Typically \fBsource\fR will be a remote source archive followed by local
patches, and configuration files.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBbuilddir\fR
.RS 4
Specifies the directory where the source code of the package will
be built.\& The default value is \fI$srcdir/$pkgname-$pkgver\fR.\& The
directory should either be \fBsrcdir\fR or be inside of \fBsrcdir\fR.\& The
directory must exist before \fBdefault_prepare\fR is called.\& This should
be set to a value that avoids usage of \fBcd\fR and absolute paths in
\fBbuild\fR, \fBcheck\fR and \fBpackage\fR if the default value is not adequate.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBoptions\fR
.RS 4
List of options for the entire \fBAPKBUILD\fR.\& Each option should be
in the list below or contain exactly one \fI:\fR to indicate that it is a
custom option.\& Options may be set for a specific (sub)packages by using
\fIsubpkgname::option\fR (or \fIsubpkgname:option\fR for custom options).\&
.PP
\fB!\&archcheck\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package contains binaries that cannot
run on the target architecture.\& This is primarily used for
packages containing firmware, and should typically never
be needed.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBbigdocs\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that this packages intentionally has a large -doc subpackage.\&
Thereby suppressing a warning to be emitted if the -doc subpackage
exceeds a certain package size threshold (currently 2 MiB).\&
.PP
.RE
\fBcharset.\&alias\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package ships a \fI/usr/lib/charset.\&alias\fR
file and that it should be installed on the user'\&s
system.\& This is almost never the case.\& Do not use this option.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB!\&check\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package will not run a test suite.\& The
reason for disabling the check phase should be noted in
a comment.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBcheckroot\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that this package'\&s test suite will be run in
fakeroot(8).\& This is necessary for some test suites which
fail when run as non-root.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB!\&dbg\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package should not be built with a debug
information package.\& This option only has an effect when DEFAULT_DBG
has been set for abuild(1) and the package is not \fBnoarch\fR.\& This
may be used for packages with signed binaries.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB!\&fhs\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package violates FHS and installs to a
location such as \fI/usr/local\fR, \fI/opt\fR, or \fI/srv\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBldpath-recursive\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that abuild(1) should use the \fB--recursive\fR argument
to scanelf(1) when attempting to find shared library (.\&so)
dependencies for the package.\& See also \fBldpath\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBlib64\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package installs files under \fI/lib64\fR
or \fI/usr/lib64\fR and that the test for those directories
should be skipped.\& This is discouraged and should only be
used for packages providing compatibility for GNU libc.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBlibtool\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package requires its libtool (.\&la)
files.\& They will not be automatically removed by abuild(1).\&
.PP
.RE
\fBnet\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package build system requires access
to a network.\& This is discouraged and an issue should be
filed with the package'\&s authors.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB!\&strip\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that strip(1) should not be run on any of the
package'\&s binaries.\& In abuild(1) this is implied if the DEBUG
variable is set.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsuid\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that binaries in the package may be installed
set-uid.\& This is a security risk and it is highly recommended
to use capabilities or process separation instead of set-uid
where available.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsetcap\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that binaries in the package may be installed
with extra setcap(8) capabilities.\& If this option is enabled,
it is highly recommended to only make these binaries executable
by root and users of a specific group, not by others.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBtextrels\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package'\&s binaries are known to contain
relocations against text segments.\& By default, abuild(1)
will refuse to create such a package because this is a
security concern.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBtoolchain\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that the package is part of the base toolchain
set and may depend on packages like \fIg++\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB!\&tracedeps\fR
.RS 4
Specifies that abuild(1) should not automatically populate
\fBdepends\fR and \fBprovides\fR with shared library (.\&so), symlink
target dependencies and similar.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsover-namecheck\fR
.RS 4
May be removed in the future.\&
.PP
.RE
.RE
\fBldpath\fR
.RS 4
Colon seperated list of directory paths that should be searched for
shared objects.\& These are not searched recursively unless the
\fBldpath-recursive\fR option is specified.\&
abuild(1) always searches \fI/usr/lib\fR, \fI/lib\fR and paths referenced by
DT_RPATH/DT_RUNPATH.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsonameprefix\fR
.RS 4
Used to namespace traced soname providers: \fBso:$sonameprefix$soname\fR.\&
When set to a non-empty value it should end with a colon.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBpcprefix\fR
.RS 4
Used to namespace traced pc(5) providers: \fBpc:$pcprefix$pcname\fR.\&
When set to a non-empty value it should end with a colon.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBlangdir\fR
.RS 4
Used by \fBdefault_lang\fR to amove language files.\& Defaults to
\fB/usr/share/locale\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsomask\fR
.RS 4
List of sonames that will be ignored when tracing providers and
dependencies of shared objects.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBgiturl\fR
.RS 4
URL of the Git repository to use with `abuild snapshot`.\& If the default
branch of the repository is not desired, a different one may be specified by
appending \fB-b\fR \fIbranch\fR where \fIbranch\fR is the branch to checkout.\&
This should typically not be used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBdisturl\fR
.RS 4
URL to an rsync server which will be used by `abuild snapshot` to
upload the generated archive to.\& This should typically not be used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBreporev\fR
.RS 4
git revision that `abuild snapshot` generates the archive for.\& This
should typically not be used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsha512sums\fR
.RS 4
Variable generated by \fBabuild checksum\fR and used by \fBfetch\fR to verify
the hashes of remote files.\& It must be at the end of the \fBAPKBUILD\fR file
for \fBabuild checksum\fR to work and should not be manually written.\&
.PP
.RE
.SS Predefined Variables
.PP
The following variables are defined for you by abuild(1) and must not
be overridden or unset.\&
.PP
\fBpkgdir\fR
.RS 4
The absolute path to the directory where the built files will be
installed.\& Typically, an APKBUILD calls `make DESTDIR="$pkgdir" install`
or similar to install the files.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsrcdir\fR
.RS 4
The absolute path to the directory where the files specified in \fBsource\fR
are symlinked and unpacked to.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBtmpdir\fR
.RS 4
The absolute path to a directory that may be used for temporary files
and is automatically cleaned up.\& This should be used instead of
mktemp(1), /tmp, \fBTMPDIR\fR or similar.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBstartdir\fR
.RS 4
The absolute path to the directory where the \fBAPKBUILD\fR file resides.\&
This should normally not be used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBAPKBUILD\fR
.RS 4
Specifies the absolute path of the \fBAPKBUILD\fR file itself.\& This should
normally not be used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsubpkgname\fR
.RS 4
Specifies the name of the current subpackage.\& This variable is only
set when a split function is invoked.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsubpkgdir\fR
.RS 4
Specifies the directory where the subpackage'\&s files should be
placed.\& This variable is only set when a split function is invoked.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsubpkgarch\fR
.RS 4
The architecture for the current subpackage.\& This variable is
only set when a split function is invoked.\& This should normally not be
used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsubpkgtriplet\fR
.RS 4
The triplet that the current subpackage is for.\& This variable is
only set when a split function is invoked.\& This should normally not be
used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBCBUILD\fR, \fBCHOST\fR, \fBCTARGET\fR
.RS 4
Triplets of machines.\& See abuild(1) for more details.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBCBUILD_ARCH\fR, \fBCARCH\fR, \fBCTARGET_ARCH\fR
.RS 4
Specifies the architecture of \fBCBUILD\fR, \fBCHOST\fR, \fBCTARGET\fR respectively.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBBOOTSTRAP\fR
.RS 4
See abuild(1).\& This should normally not be used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBSOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\fR
.RS 4
Timestamp for reproducible builds.\& When \fBprepare\fR, \fBbuild\fR,
\fBcheck\fR, \fBpackage\fR or split function is invoked this is always set.\&
This variable is always exported.\&
.PP
.RE
.SS Functions
.PP
Functions specified here may be implemented in the global environment.\&
An \fBAPKBUILD\fR may implement other functions but their names should start
with an underscore.\& Between external invocation of functions the shell
environment may be reset to the global environment.\&
.PP
\fBfetch\fR
.RS 4
The default implementation is a single call to \fBdefault_fetch\fR.\& This
function should not be overwritten.\& Instead use \fBprepare\fR and \fBnet\fR
in \fBoptions\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBunpack\fR
.RS 4
The default implementation is a single call to \fBdefault_unpack\fR.\& This
function should not be overwritten.\& Instead use \fBprepare\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBprepare\fR
.RS 4
Prepares the source in \fBsrcdir\fR and \fBbuilddir\fR to be built.\& The default
implementation is a single call of \fBdefault_prepare\fR.\& You must call
\fBdefault_prepare\fR if you write a custom \fBprepare\fR function.\&
.PP
A custom \fBprepare\fR function may call \fBupdate_config_sub\fR or
\fBupdate_config_guess\fR to update \fBconfig.\&sub\fR and \fBconfig.\&guess\fR files
respectively.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBbuild\fR
.RS 4
Compiles the source in \fBbuilddir\fR.\& The default implementation does
nothing.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBcheck\fR
.RS 4
Runs the package'\&s test suite.\& This function must be implemented unless
\fB!\&check\fR was specified in \fBoptions\fR.\& It may be implemented even when
\fB!\&check\fR is specified in \fBoptions\fR.\& Whether or not this function is
run should not effect the resulting package.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBpackage\fR
.RS 4
Install files from \fBsrcdir\fR or \fBbuilddir\fR to \fBpkgdir\fR.\& This function
must be implemented.\& Note that \fBpkgdir\fR is not created for you; if
this package installs no files (for example, a metapackage), you must
use `mkdir -p "$pkgdir"`.\& If mandatory the \fBpackage\fR function may
append generated dependencies or providers to \fBdepends\fR, \fBprovides\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBsplit functions\fR
.RS 4
Functions with names as specified by \fBsubpackages\fR.\& It should move
files from \fBpkgdir\fR, \fBsrcdir\fR or \fBbuilddir\fR to \fBsubpkgdir\fR.\& The default
working directory is \fBpkgdir\fR.\& It must create \fBsubpkgdir\fR.\& A split
function may set \fBpkgdesc\fR, \fBurl\fR, \fBlicense\fR, \fBdepends\fR, \fBinstall_if\fR,
\fBprovides\fR, \fBprovider_priority\fR, \fBreplaces\fR, \fBreplaces_priority\fR to
change the value for the current subpackage.\& By default \fBprovides\fR
and \fBinstall_if\fR is emptied for subpackages, \fBdepends\fR may be emptied
in the future.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBlang\fR, \fBdoc\fR, \fBdbg\fR, \fBdev\fR, \fBstatic\fR, \fBlibs\fR, \fBopenrc\fR, \fBsystemd\fR,
\fBudev\fR, \fBdevhelp\fR, \fBbashcomp\fR, \fBzshcomp\fR, \fBfishcomp\fR, \fBpyc\fR, \fBnftables\fR
.RS 4
Special split functions in the sense that they have a default
implementation that call \fBdefault_<name>\fR.\& These should typically not
need to be overwritten.\&
.PP
.RE
.SS Predefined Functions
.PP
The following functions may be (directly or indirectly) used in an
\fBAPKBUILD\fR but must not be overwritten or unset.\&
.PP
\fBdefault_fetch\fR
.RS 4
When called in \fBfetch\fR it fetches remote files specified in \fBsource\fR
and verifies their hashes with \fBsha512sums\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBdefault_unpack\fR
.RS 4
When called in \fBunpack\fR it unpacks archives from \fBsource\fR into \fBsrcdir\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBdefault_prepare\fR
.RS 4
When called in \fBprepare\fR it changes the working directory to \fBbuilddir\fR
and applies all \fI*.\&patch\fR, \fI*.\&patch.\&gz\fR and \fI*.\&patch.\&xz\fR files
specified in \fBsource\fR.\& Implementation may differ slightly.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBwant_check\fR
.RS 4
Returns 0 if the \fBcheck\fR function will be run and 1 otherwise.\& The
return value should not effect the resulting packages.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBamove\fR
.RS 4
Inside of split functions this moves the given file hierarchies from
\fBpkgdir\fR or the current working directory to \fBsubpkgdir\fR, creating
directories as needed.\& The created directories may not have the same
attributes as the original ones.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBdefault_lang\fR, \fBdefault_doc\fR, \fBdefault_dbg\fR, \fBdefault_dev\fR,
\fBdefault_static\fR, \fBdefault_libs\fR, \fBdefault_openrc\fR, \fBdefault_systemd\fR,
\fBdefault_udev\fR, \fBdefault_devhelp\fR, \fBdefault_bashcomp\fR, \fBdefault_zshcomp\fR,
\fBdefault_fishcomp\fR, \fBdefault_pyc\fR, \fBdefault_nftables\fR
.RS 4
Move common files and set common variables in a split function.\&
\fBdepends\fR is typically set to the value of \fBdepends_${name#default_}\fR
(eg.\& \fBdefault_lang\fR uses \fBdepends="$depends_lang"\fR).\& They may override
or change variables set in the split function before calling \fBdefault_*\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
.SS Install Scripts
.PP
An install script is run when an action is taken on a package by apk(8).\& An
install script must be written in shell and must have a \fI#!\&/bin/sh\fR
interpreter declaration as the first line.\& The \fBinstall\fR variable must
contain the install scripts needed by the package.\&
.PP
The install script will be run inside the root filesystem where the package
is being installed.\& A single argument will be passed to call scripts, which
is the version of the package being currently installed (or deinstalled).\& The
pre-upgrade and post-upgrade scripts will have an additional second argument,
which specifies the version of the package before the upgrade process.\&
.PP
The different actions that may have install scripts specified are as follows:
.PP
\fB$pkgname.\&pre-install\fR
.RS 4
Executed before the package is installed.\& If this script exits with
an error (non-zero exit code), apk(8) will halt the installation and
the package will not be installed.\& This install script is typically
used to create any users or groups needed as described in \fBpkggroups\fR
and \fBpkgusers\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB$pkgname.\&post-install\fR
.RS 4
Executed after the package is installed.\& If this script exits
with an error (non-zero exit code), apk(8) will mark the package as
broken.\& The `apk fix` command will attempt to re-run the post-install
script if this occurs.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB$pkgname.\&pre-upgrade\fR
.RS 4
Executed before the package is upgraded.\& If this script exits with
an error (non-zero exit code), apk(8) will mark the package as broken.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB$pkgname.\&post-upgrade\fR
.RS 4
Executed after the package is upgraded.\& If this script exits with
an error (non-zero exit code), apk(8) will mark the package as
broken.\& The `apk fix` command will attempt to re-run the post-upgrade
script if this occurs.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB$pkgname.\&pre-deinstall\fR
.RS 4
Executed before the package is removed from the system.\& If this
script exits with an error (non-zero exit code), apk(8) will not
remove the package from the system.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB$pkgname.\&post-deinstall\fR
.RS 4
Executed after the package is removed from the system.\& Exiting with
an error will have no effect.\&
.PP
.PP
.RE
